  content   = '> **Quick Answer:** White collar crime in Bangladesh covers economically motivated, non-violent offences: fraud, money laundering, corruption, and securities violations, prosecuted under five statutes including the ACC Act 2004 and MLPA 2012. Bangladesh''s ACC holds a 48.17% conviction rate in such cases and the BFIU has frozen Tk 76,000 crore in financial crime proceeds.

## What Are White Collar Crimes in Bangladesh?

White collar crimes in Bangladesh are economically motivated, non-violent offences committed by individuals or corporations occupying positions of trust. The concept originates in Edwin Sutherland''s 1939 criminological framework, but Bangladesh''s legal system addresses them through five operative statutes: the Anti-Corruption Commission Act 2004, the Money Laundering Prevention Act 2012 (MLPA), the Securities and Exchange Ordinance 1969, the Bank Company Act 1991, and the Penal Code 1860. Four regulators share jurisdiction: the ACC, the Bangladesh Financial Intelligence Unit (BFIU), the Bangladesh Securities and Exchange Commission (BSEC), and Bangladesh Bank.

Global Financial Integrity estimates that approximately $150 billion was illicitly transferred out of Bangladesh over the last decade, with annual outflows reaching $12 billion. In 2024, the Anti-Corruption Commission recorded a conviction rate of 48.17% (an eight-year low), while 3,410 cases remained pending across special tribunals. White collar crimes in Bangladesh expose a stark contradiction: the country possesses robust statutes for prosecution, yet its enforcement machinery consistently fails to deliver accountability.

### Quick Reference: Penalties Under Bangladesh White Collar Crime Laws

| Offence | Statute | Maximum Imprisonment | Maximum Fine |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cheating | Penal Code s.415 | 7 years | Court discretion |
| Criminal breach of trust | Penal Code s.406 | 7 years | Court discretion |
| Forgery | Penal Code s.463 | 7 years | Court discretion |
| Money laundering | MLPA 2012 s.4(2) | 12 years | 2x asset value or Tk 20 lakh |
| Insider trading | Securities Ordinance 1969 | 5 years | Tk 5 crore |
| Securities fraud | Securities Ordinance 1969 | 5 years | Tk 5 crore |
| Banking offences | Bank Company Act s.109 | 7 years | Tk 50 lakh |
| Wilful default | Bank Company Act s.27B | — | Tk 1 crore + Tk 1 lakh/day |
| Corruption | ACC Act 2004 | 10 years | Court discretion |

## The Statutory Architecture: Five Statutes That Define White Collar Crime in Bangladesh

Most textbooks catalogue types of white collar crime without linking them to specific statutory provisions. That approach fails both in examinations and in court. What follows is a provision-level map of Bangladeshi white collar criminal law.

### The Penal Code 1860 as Foundation

The Penal Code 1860 remains the residual criminal statute. Section 25 defines "fraudulently" as acting with intent to defraud. Section 406 criminalises criminal breach of trust (three to seven years). Section 415 defines cheating (one to seven years). Sections 421 through 424 address fraudulent dispositions of property to defeat creditors: provisions that frequently arise alongside land disputes, as explored in our guide to the [Transfer of Property Act 1882](/articles/transfer-of-property-act-1882-sections-3-41-bangladesh). Section 463 criminalises forgery (up to seven years). Prosecutors frequently charge Penal Code offences alongside specialised statutes to ensure fallback convictions.

### Money Laundering Prevention Act 2012

The MLPA 2012 is Bangladesh''s primary anti-laundering instrument. Section 4(2) prescribes four to twelve years'' imprisonment plus a fine equal to double the asset value or BDT 2 million, whichever is greater. Section 4(3) mandates state forfeiture of laundered property. The Act enumerates 27 predicate offences in its schedule.

Section 12 imposes a critical procedural gate: no court may take cognizance of a money laundering offence without prior approval from the [Bangladesh Financial Intelligence Unit (BFIU)](https://bfiu.bangladesh.gov.bd). This provision centralises prosecutorial control but also creates a bottleneck. Where BFIU approval is withheld or delayed, otherwise meritorious cases stall indefinitely.

### Anti-Corruption Commission Act 2004

The [ACC Act 2004](https://acc.org.bd) establishes the ACC as the sole nationwide authority to investigate scheduled corruption offences (s.20(1)). Section 26 empowers the ACC to issue asset declaration notices compelling disclosure of assets and liabilities. Section 32 requires a prosecution sanction (Form No. 3) before filing a charge sheet: a requirement intended to prevent frivolous prosecution that has also served as a mechanism for political interference. In *Tarique Rahman vs Government* (63 DLR (AD) 18), the Appellate Division confirmed the ACC''s prosecution role over money laundering under ACC Act s.9.

### Securities and Exchange Ordinance 1969 and Bank Company Act 1991

Orders 19 and 19A of the Securities and Exchange Ordinance 1969 prohibit insider trading, with penalties up to five years'' imprisonment or Tk 5 crore. BSEC exercises regulatory enforcement, referring criminal cases to the justice system. In 2019, BSEC fined Tk 2.29 crore on 16 persons and 3 firms, and suspended 3 stockbroker licences.

Section 109 of the Bank Company Act 1991 punishes banking offences with up to seven years'' imprisonment and fines between Tk 5 lakh and Tk 50 lakh. Section 27B introduces wilful defaulter provisions: automatic directorship vacancy, fines between Tk 50 lakh and Tk 1 crore, and a continuing default penalty of Tk 1 lakh per day. Wilful defaulters also face consequences under insolvency frameworks: examined in our guide on [declaring bankruptcy in Bangladesh](/articles/declaring-bankruptcy-bangladesh-debt-relief).

## Landmark Cases: How Courts Have Treated White Collar Offences

One case produced life sentences. Another, involving $667 million in losses, produced zero prosecutions. The divergence reveals everything about Bangladeshi enforcement.

### The Hallmark-Sonali Bank Scam (Tk 3,500 Crore)

Between 2010 and 2012, Hallmark Group extracted approximately Tk 3,500 crore through fraudulent letter-of-credit-backed lending from Sonali Bank''s Ruposhi Bangla branch using fabricated export documentation and complicit bank officials. In March 2024, a special tribunal delivered life imprisonment for Hallmark Managing Director Tanvir Mahmud and his wife Jasmine Islam: the highest sentence ever imposed for a white collar offence in Bangladesh. Nine individuals were convicted and each fined Tk 50 million under Penal Code s.406, s.420, and the MLPA 2012. This verdict stands as proof that the statutory framework can produce serious consequences when political conditions permit prosecution.

### The 2010-11 Stock Market Scam and ACC Jurisprudence

The Dhaka Stock Exchange fell 1,800 points (21%) between December 2010 and January 2011, inflicting BDT 50 billion ($667 million) in retail investor losses. A government inquiry named 60 individuals including a former DSE president and a former SEC chairman. Zero criminal prosecutions followed: political protection and regulatory capture meant the country''s most consequential securities fraud went entirely unpunished.

The Appellate Division has developed procedural limits on the ACC. In *Obaidul Kader vs State* (63 DLR 425), the court held that ACC discretionary powers must be exercised transparently with recorded reasons. In *ACC vs Dr Mohiuddin Khan Alamgir* (62 DLR (AD) 290), the Appellate Division invalidated mechanical asset declaration notices. *ACC vs Shamima Begum* (62 DLR (AD) 277) established that abetment charges require substantive evidence. These decisions confirm that statutory tools exist: but procedural compliance and political will determine whether they produce justice.

## Criminal and Civil Remedies for White Collar Crime in Bangladesh

### Criminal Penalties

The penalty structure spans all five statutes (see Quick Reference table above). All ACC-prosecuted offences require prosecution sanction under s.32 (Form No. 3) before a charge sheet is filed. Where sanction is withheld, complete investigations die on the file. The Securities Ordinance permits administrative penalties directly from BSEC without criminal prosecution. Bank Company Act s.27B creates regulatory consequences: directorship removal and daily fines: that operate independently of any criminal charge.

### Civil Remedies

The [Contract Act 1872](/articles/contract-act-1872-bangladesh-explained-practitioners-guide) provides the primary civil framework. Section 17 defines fraud through five constituent acts: false statement of fact, active concealment, promise without intention to perform, any other act fitted to deceive, and any act law declares fraudulent. Section 19 renders fraud-induced contracts voidable with full restitution. The Specific Relief Act 1877 s.35 empowers courts to rescind fraudulent contracts. Civil damages in tort for deceit remain available. For complex financial disputes, [alternative dispute resolution](/articles/adr-common-law-countries-legislative-review) may offer a faster path than protracted litigation.

The BFIU can freeze transactions without a court order under MLPA 2012 s.23(1)(c). BSEC may suspend trading and deregister market intermediaries. Bangladesh Bank may revoke banking licences and remove management.

### The Missing Remedy: Victim Compensation

Bangladesh has no dedicated victim compensation fund for white collar crime. A UNAFEI study concluded that Bangladeshi law does not appropriately compensate victims. Victims must pursue ad-valorem fee-bearing civil suits while the state retains all criminal fines. The absence of a victim compensation mechanism is not merely a policy gap: it is a structural injustice. In every other Commonwealth jurisdiction that Bangladesh draws its legal heritage from, criminal courts retain the power to order compensation directly to victims as part of sentencing. Bangladesh has no equivalent provision for white collar offences. The fines collected from Hallmark defendants (Tk 50 million each) went to the state, not to the depositors whose funds were looted. Reform proposals include a dedicated restitution fund financed from criminal fines and forfeited assets, but no legislation has been tabled.

This policy gap leaves retail investors who lost savings in the 2010-11 stock market crash, and depositors defrauded in banking scams, without meaningful direct redress. Criminal and civil tracks can run in parallel, but the cost of civil litigation effectively bars most victims from recovery.

## Corporate Liability and the Doctrinal Gap

Part XI of the Companies Act 1994 criminalises fraudulent trading. Directors owe fiduciary duties as trustees of corporate assets, and breach carries both civil and criminal consequences. Courts retain the power to pierce the corporate veil when a company is used as a fraud vehicle. The MLPA 2012 does not distinguish between natural and juristic persons; s.4 applies to corporate persons. The ACC Act''s reference to "any person" has been interpreted to include companies.

Bangladeshi courts apply the "directing mind and will" doctrine to attribute individual conduct to the corporation: the same principle applied in English company law. The MLPA 2012 does not distinguish between natural and juristic persons; s.4 applies to corporate persons without qualification. Bank Company Act s.27B treats wilful default as grounds for automatic directorship vacancy. The ACC Act''''s reference to "any person" has been interpreted to include companies by the Appellate Division.

In practice, no reported Bangladeshi case exists in which a corporation: as distinct from its directors: received a criminal conviction for a white collar offence. Bangladeshi courts overwhelmingly prefer individual prosecution, reflecting both doctrinal conservatism and practical difficulty in attributing criminal intent to an entity.

Bangladesh has no equivalent of the UK Bribery Act s.7 "failure to prevent" offence, no deferred prosecution agreements, and no corporate sentencing guidelines. The Hallmark verdict sent individuals to prison for life while the corporate structure that enabled the fraud continued operating. Until dedicated corporate criminal liability provisions are enacted, white collar enforcement will remain person-focused and structurally incomplete.

## Enforcement Realities: Why White Collar Convictions Remain Rare in Bangladesh

The ACC''s 2024 conviction rate of 48.17% was an eight-year low. With 3,410 cases pending and 965 stayed across court levels, the enforcement system is failing by its own metrics.

In 2024 the ACC received 15,842 complaints, accepted only 1,894 (11.95%), filed 451 cases, and submitted 403 charge sheets. The 48.17% conviction rate means more than half of prosecuted cases ended in acquittal. Approximately 90% of pre-August 2024 complaints were abandoned without meaningful investigation. The nationwide case backlog stands at 4.65 million cases. Judicial independence is compromised by the Ministry of Law''s control over judicial postings and transfers. The ACC remains understaffed and lacks forensic accounting capacity for complex financial crime.

The BFIU is a relative bright spot: 30,199 STRs/SARs in FY2024-25 (a 74% increase), and Tk 76,000 crore in frozen assets. As a member of the Egmont Group of 170+ financial intelligence units, the BFIU can share intelligence across borders. But freezing assets is not convicting offenders. The gap between BFIU output and courtroom outcomes remains the central enforcement failure.

Over 45% of significant white collar cases experience unjustified prosecution delays. Forensic accounting expertise is scarce: complex financial crimes require specialist investigators that the ACC does not possess in sufficient numbers. Expert witnesses capable of reconstructing multi-layered financial transactions are rare, and their testimony is costly. These structural deficiencies mean that even when political will exists, the institutional machinery frequently cannot deliver prosecutable evidence.

Following the August 2024 political transition, the ACC filed 399 new cases against former Awami League figures. This surge correlates with regime change, not institutional reform: a pattern that risks replicating the selective justice it purports to correct.

## International Asset Recovery: UNCAC and the $12 Billion Challenge

[Transparency International Bangladesh](https://www.transparency.org/en/countries/bangladesh)''s 2023 UNCAC parallel report recorded "very little achievement in terms of asset recovery" despite $12 billion in estimated annual outflows. Bangladesh ratified UNCAC in 2007, has 81 bilateral mutual legal assistance agreements, and MLPA s.4(3) permits forfeiture orders extending to foreign-held assets: yet recovery remains minimal.

Shell companies and layered ownership structures obscure beneficial ownership across jurisdictions. Dual criminality requirements add further barriers: the predicate offence must be criminal in both the requesting and requested state. Former Land Minister Saifuzzaman Chowdhury allegedly accumulated $675 million across the UK, US, Dubai, Malaysia, and Singapore: the dispersal across multiple jurisdictions multiplying legal complexity. The same concealment structures appear in fraudulent [land registration transactions](/articles/land-registration-process-bangladesh-step-by-step), where fabricated ownership documents hide the true beneficial owner.

Successive governments have lacked the political will to pursue predecessors'''' assets, because doing so establishes a precedent that applies equally to their own conduct. Foreign financial institutions: particularly in the UK, UAE, and Singapore: have repeatedly failed due diligence obligations that should have flagged politically exposed persons. Bangladesh''''s membership in the Egmont Group provides the intelligence exchange infrastructure; what is missing is the prosecutorial will to use it.

The UNCAC review mechanism has recommended a dedicated asset recovery office and beneficial ownership registries. Civil forfeiture: requiring a lower evidentiary standard than criminal prosecution: offers the most practical path forward.

