## How to File a White Collar Crime Complaint in Bangladesh

The ACC accepts complaints in person at any district office, by post to 1 Segunbagicha Dhaka, or online at acc.org.bd. Your complaint should include your contact details, the accused''s name and designation, specific offence details (dates, amounts, locations), supporting documents, and any prior complaints filed elsewhere. Of 15,842 complaints in 2024, only 1,894 were accepted for investigation. If accepted, an inquiry officer is assigned; sufficient evidence triggers an FIR; a charge sheet requires prosecution sanction under s.32 before reaching a special tribunal. Follow up in writing every 30 days and retain copies of all correspondence.

For money laundering, report to the BFIU at bfiu.bangladesh.gov.bd: early reporting is critical as the BFIU can freeze assets immediately under MLPA s.23(1)(c). For securities fraud, file with BSEC directly. For civil relief, a civil suit requires no regulatory pre-approval. Note: direct criminal complaints to magistrates for ACC-scheduled or money laundering offences will be returned: court cognizance requires ACC sanction (s.32) or BFIU approval (MLPA s.12) first.

## Whistleblower Protection: A Critical Gap

Bangladesh has no dedicated whistleblower protection statute. ACC Act s.20(3) gives only narrow defamation immunity to informants: it does not cover retaliation, job loss, or physical threats. The Right to Information Act 2009 offers limited shelter for public officials. No provision in the MLPA 2012 or Securities Ordinance protects private sector employees who report employer fraud.

This gap directly undermines enforcement. Employees with direct knowledge of financial fraud: the most valuable witnesses in white collar prosecutions: have no legal shield if they come forward. The 90% complaint abandonment rate before August 2024 reflects, in part, the rational calculation that reporting carries personal risk without legal protection. UNCAC Article 33 requires states to protect witnesses from retaliation; Bangladesh''s compliance remains incomplete. The ACC Reform Commission established after August 2024 has identified whistleblower legislation as a priority reform. Until enacted, individuals should consult legal counsel before filing and report through lawyers or civil society organisations where possible.

## Frequently Asked Questions: White Collar Crimes in Bangladesh

### What is the legal definition of white collar crime in Bangladesh?

No statutory definition exists. Bangladeshi law addresses specific offences: fraud (Penal Code s.25), money laundering (MLPA 2012 s.4), securities fraud (Securities and Exchange Ordinance 1969), and corruption (ACC Act 2004). The category is doctrinal, not legislative.

### Which body investigates white collar crimes in Bangladesh?

The ACC holds sole nationwide authority for scheduled corruption offences. The BFIU handles money laundering. BSEC regulates securities fraud. Bangladesh Bank supervises banking offences. Jurisdiction depends on the offence charged.

### What is the maximum punishment for money laundering in Bangladesh?

MLPA 2012 s.4(2) prescribes four to twelve years'' imprisonment plus a fine equal to double the laundered asset value or BDT 2 million, whichever is greater. Section 4(3) mandates state forfeiture of laundered property. These penalties apply to both natural and juristic persons.

### Can a corporation be criminally liable in Bangladesh?

Yes in statute: Companies Act 1994 Part XI criminalises fraudulent trading and the MLPA applies to juristic persons. In practice, corporate prosecutions are extremely rare; courts overwhelmingly prosecute individual directors and officers instead.

### What civil remedies are available to victims of white collar crime?

Victims may seek contract rescission (Specific Relief Act s.35), restitution (Contract Act s.19), and civil damages for deceit. BFIU transaction freezes under MLPA s.23(1)(c) can preserve assets pending litigation. No dedicated victim compensation fund exists in Bangladesh.

### Has Bangladesh recovered any assets from abroad?

Minimal recovery despite UNCAC ratification in 2007 and 81 mutual legal assistance agreements. Transparency International Bangladesh''s 2023 report recorded "very little achievement" against $12 billion in estimated annual outflows. Shell companies, dual criminality barriers, and lack of political will remain the core obstacles.

### What is the difference between the ACC and the BFIU?

The ACC investigates and prosecutes scheduled corruption offences under ACC Act 2004. The BFIU collects and analyses suspicious transaction reports under MLPA 2012 and must approve money laundering prosecutions under s.12 before a court can take cognizance. Both institutions operate with different mandates and varying degrees of independence.',
